---
import PageLayout from "../../layouts/PageLayout.astro";
import NewsCards from "../../components/NewsCards.astro";
import UpcomingSessions from "../../components/UpcomingSessions.astro";
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
---

<PageLayout
  title="For educators"
  description="LLMs Unplugged for teachers: which lesson fits your year level and period length, what to print, where it sits in the Australian Curriculum, and accredited professional learning."
>
  <h1>For educators</h1>

  <p>
    If your students are asking you about AI (they are), you can answer by
    building a language model with them, on paper. The activities suit upper
    primary through secondary, in any discipline: no coding, no software to vet,
    no accounts, and no maths beyond counting and percentages. One lesson is
    enough for you and your students to see how it works.
  </p>

  <h2 id="which-lesson">Which lesson?</h2>

  <p>
    Every <a href="/lessons/">lesson</a> ships its slides with full presenter notes,
    so you can run it without having seen it delivered. Pick by your group and the
    time you have:
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Your group</th>
          <th>Time</th>
          <th>Run this</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>Years 5&ndash;6</td>
          <td>90 minutes</td>
          <td>
            <a href="/lessons/how-ai-writes-stories/">How AI writes stories</a>
            &mdash; paper cutouts from a picture book, no dice; an AI writes the class
            a story first, then they find out how
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Years 7 and up</td>
          <td>a double period (60 min)</td>
          <td>
            <a href="/lessons/my-first-language-model/"
              >My First Language Model</a
            > (60-minute version) &mdash; grid paper and a d10; maps straight onto
            probability
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Years 7 and up, or staff PD</td>
          <td>90 minutes to 2 hours</td>
          <td>
            <a href="/lessons/my-first-language-model/"
              >My First Language Model</a
            > (90-minute or 2-hour version) &mdash; adds a printed pre-trained model,
            then agents
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>A big class, or a half-hour slot</td>
          <td>10&ndash;30 minutes</td>
          <td>
            <a href="/modules/search-sheets/">Search Sheets</a> &mdash; the whole
            class runs one model by show of hands; nothing to cut
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Years 9&ndash;10, a half day</td>
          <td>3 hours</td>
          <td>
            <a href="/lessons/build-break-extend/">Build, break, extend</a>
            &mdash; cutouts, sycophancy, tool use and a poetry slam
          </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Single periods across a unit</td>
          <td>20&ndash;30 minutes each</td>
          <td>
            The <a href="/modules/">modules</a>: Training, then Generation, then
            extensions by year band. Each card shows who it suits, how long it
            takes and what it runs on
          </td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="what-to-print">What to print</h2>

  <p>
    Everything is on the <a href="/tools/">Materials</a> page, ready to print:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      the <a href="/tools/#worksheets">bigram grid</a> (A3) for the grid lessons,
      one per pair
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="/tools/#cutouts">token cutouts</a> from three picture books for the
      cutouts lessons&mdash;print double-sided and, if you can, pre-cut them: scissor
      time is the biggest schedule risk with younger groups
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="/tools/#search-sheets">search sheets</a>, one per student, for
      the whole-class version
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="/tools/#booklets-for-dice-generation">pre-trained booklets</a>
      for the 90-minute lesson and up
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Or <a href="/tools/#generate-your-own">generate your own</a> from any text your
    class already knows&mdash;the school values, a class novel, a poem they've studied.
    It runs in the browser and nothing leaves your machine.
  </p>

  <h2 id="curriculum">Where it fits in the curriculum</h2>

  <p>
    The activities fit in more than one learning area: rigorous enough to count
    as Digital Technologies, and legible to Mathematics (probability and
    statistics), English (language and text structure) and the general
    capabilities. We've mapped every module to Australian Curriculum v9.0
    content descriptions, with three suggested programs for Years 5&ndash;6,
    7&ndash;8 and 9&ndash;10:
  </p>

  <Callout type="info" title="Curriculum mapping (draft)">
    <p>
      <a href="/educators/curriculum/"
        >Mapping LLMs Unplugged to the Australian Curriculum v9.0</a
      > &mdash; the full draft, module by module, with coverage summaries by strand.
      It hasn't yet been reviewed by an external curriculum expert, so treat the codes
      as a starting point and
      <a href="/about/#get-in-touch">tell us</a> where you'd map differently. There's
      also a shorter
      <a href="/news/2026-03-18-curriculum-mapping-example/"
        >worked example for Year 5/6</a
      >.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="professional-learning">Professional learning</h2>

  <p>
    <em>LLMs Unplugged</em> is
    <a href="/news/2026-04-24-tqi-accreditation/"
      >accredited by the ACT Teacher Quality Institute</a
    > as a two-hour professional learning program, so attending a workshop counts
    toward your registered PL hours. We run open sessions at ANU in Canberra:
  </p>

  <UpcomingSessions />

  <p>
    We also deliver sessions for schools, regions and teacher networks, and
    train teachers to run the lessons themselves&mdash;<a
      href="mailto:ben.swift@anu.edu.au">get in touch</a
    > to arrange one. If you're making the case to a PL coordinator, the
    <a href="/blurbs/">workshop blurbs</a> page has descriptions at several lengths.
  </p>

  <h2 id="why-unplugged">Why unplugged?</h2>

  <p>
    Because a model you built yourself on a sheet of A3 has no black box in it:
    every step is visible, no data leaves the room, and nothing talks back. It
    gets you the understanding the
    <a href="/news/2026-03-20-ai-framework-without-the-burden/"
      >AI schools framework</a
    > asks for. The longer arguments:
  </p>

  <NewsCards kinds={["essay"]} />

  <h2 id="in-classrooms">In classrooms</h2>

  <p>
    Where it's been run, and what happened&mdash;from a Year 5 class at Duffy
    Primary to a pre-service teacher's Year 7/8 class with a Yoda monologue for
    training text:
  </p>

  <NewsCards kinds={["report"]} />

  <h2 id="tell-us">Tell us how it went</h2>

  <p>
    If you've run these activities, adapted them, or mapped them to a different
    year level or syllabus, we'd love to hear about it&mdash;the search sheets
    exist because a Queensland teacher wrote to say her lessons had been cut to
    thirty minutes. <a href="mailto:ben.swift@anu.edu.au"
      >ben.swift@anu.edu.au</a
    >.
  </p>
</PageLayout>

<style>
  .table-wrap {
    overflow-x: auto;
    margin: 1rem 0 1.5rem;
  }

  td:first-child {
    white-space: nowrap;
  }
</style>
